// [expr.ass]/1: "the left operand shall be a modifiable lvalue".
// Nothing in typechecking asked. The only thing that rejected
// `(a + b) = 5;` was codegen's "expression is not assignable" -- which
// was simultaneously the *reference-binding* rejection, and that second
// use was wrong ([conv.rval] materializes a prvalue bound to a
// `const T&` rather than refusing it). Separating the two questions put
// this rule in the pass that decides it and let the binding materialize;
// without the separation, making the binding work would have made this
// assignment silently write into a temporary.
import std;

int main() {
    int64_t a = 1;
    int64_t b = 2;
    (a + b) = 5;
    return 0;
}
